Title:
RawHive — Cobalt Strike BOF for raw NTFS hive extraction

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- RawHive is a Cobalt Strike Beacon Object File (BOF) that extracts sensitive Windows registry hives (SAM/SYSTEM/SECURITY) and optionally `ntds.dit` directly from a raw NTFS volume.  
- It avoids standard Windows file APIs by opening the volume device (e.g., `\\.\C:`), parsing NTFS metadata, locating the target file records, and reading file bytes straight from disk.  
- The tool walks directory indexes to resolve paths and falls back to a bounded MFT scan if needed, then reconstructs file content via resident attributes or runlists.  
- Exfiltration can be done via BeaconDownload (no on-disk artifacts) or by writing `.tmp` files to a specified directory (less OPSEC-safe).  
- It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters operating as local admin/SYSTEM who want to collect credential material while reducing user-mode API telemetry.  
- Interesting due to its direct NTFS parsing approach, safety bounds (scan/runlist caps), and BOF-friendly streaming over C2.

Technical Focus:
- Raw disk/volume access via `\\.\<drive>:` device paths
- NTFS on-disk structures: boot sector, `$MFT`, directory indexes
- MFT record parsing and bounded scanning
- NTFS data attributes: resident data and non-resident runlists (cluster extents)
- Cobalt Strike BOF + BeaconDownload-based exfiltration

Use Cases:
- Dump SAM/SYSTEM/SECURITY for offline hash extraction and local credential recovery
- Extract `ntds.dit` from domain controllers for offline AD credential analysis
- Collect locked/in-use files without relying on normal file read APIs
- Reduce endpoint telemetry associated with standard file access during collection
- Post-compromise credential access workflows integrated into Cobalt Strike

Keywords:
Cobalt Strike, BOF, BeaconDownload, NTFS, raw volume access, \\.\C:, $MFT, MFT records, directory index, runlist, cluster extents, resident attribute, SAM, SYSTEM hive, SECURITY hive, ntds.dit, offline credential dumping, direct disk read, Windows internals, opsec